      "text": "To determine the number of rows in the display, we need to understand the pattern of the arrangement in which each row has two more cans than the row above it. The display starts with 1 can in the top row and each subsequent row has 2 more cans than the previous row. We can represent the total number of cans using an arithmetic series where the first term \\(a_1 = 1\\) and the common difference \\(d = 2\\).\n\nThe formula for the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of an arithmetic series is given by:\n\\[\nS_n = \\frac{n}{2} \\times (2a_1 + (n-1)d)\n\\]\nwhere \\(S_n\\) is the total number of cans, \\(a_1\\) is the first term, \\(d\\) is the common difference, and \\(n\\) is the number of terms.\n\nWe are given that \\(S_n = 100\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{2} \\times (2 \\times 1 + (n-1) \\times 2) = 100\n\\]\nSimplifying the expression inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{2} \\times (2 + 2n - 2) = 100\n\\]\nThis reduces to:\n\\[\n\\frac{n}{2} \\times 2n = 100\n\\]\n\\[\nn^2 = 100\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides:\n\\[\nn = \\sqrt{100} = 10\n\\]\nThus, the display contains . Answer:\n\\(\\boxed{10}\\)",
