      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "This problem is similar to a standard pattern recognition problem (see AOPS Algebra Vol 2 Chapter 2/3 Problem 1).\n\nFor example, a pine tree grows at a rate of 2 feet/week, the tree goes from 6 feet tall after one week to 8 feet tall after two weeks to 10 feet tall after three weeks. So, the tree grows ______ feet every week.\n\nPick from: (A) 1    (B) 2    (C) 3    (D) 4    (E) 5\n\nPick from: (A) 4    (B) 3    (C) 2    (D) 1    (E) 0\n\nPick from: (A) -2    (B) -1    (C) 0    (D) 1    (E) 2\n\nPick from: (A) -1    (B) 0    (C) 1    (D) 1    (E) 3\n**Step 1: Calculate the number of rows in the display.**\nLet the number of rows be 1.\nFrom the given increase in cans per row, the final cans in this case would be 100.\n\nTherefore, the number of rows in the display is $\\boxed{5}$.\n\n**Step 2: Rewrite the problem as a pattern recognition problem.**\nLet the first number of cans be 1.\nEach row after will have 2 more cans than the row before, so the total number of cans can be represented as:\n\n$1+3+5+7+9+11+13+.....+203$ total cans.\n\nOnce you realize that we are basically summing 1, 3, 5, 7, and 9, every increasing by 2, you can realize this is equivalent to an arithmetic sequence $\\frac{n}{2}(a_1+a_n)$. wherein:\n$A_1$ = first value, while $A_n$ = last value in the pattern.\n\nIn this example, our first value is 1 and 203 is the last value in the pattern when we count on by quickly adding the increments of 2 (one positioned by the question stem).\nSince ($1$ is not a multiple of 2, and 100 is not odd) we can be sure that we can solve this pattern by using the following equation: $\\frac{204}{2}-1+1=101$ which has every 10 cans but has us keeping it demod 2.\nThe answer is **C**.",
